<p>A few things to say&#8230;</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I think what you have is a bit vague. The first question I have is what is the SCP? You talk about a series of radio transmissions and then an <em>abnormal mass</em> that’s heading towards earth. Your elevator pitch makes me believe that the SCP is the radio signals, however your hook contradicts this and makes me think it is the <em>abnormal mass</em>.</p>
<p>My second question would be how does an astronomer notices this mass? You clearly state that the signals are originating from a galaxy, implying that the foundation can’t narrow it down any further than that; but then you contradict this by saying an astronomer notices a mass exiting the galaxy. For one, the astronomer would barely be able to see the galaxy through the dust plane in the Milky Way, let alone notice a mass heading towards earth, and the mass would have to be absolutely massive for the scientist to see it. And something that big would collapse in on itself due to its own gravitational pull.</p>
<p>I think you’ve got an idea, but the road that leads to this idea working, is a road towards a lot of research. Otherwise, there’s gonna be many holes that people find by simply looking it up.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

