			<div class="content" id="post-content-4828098">
									

<blockquote>
<p>The first question I have is what is the SCP? You talk about a series of radio transmissions and then an abnormal mass that’s heading towards earth. Your elevator pitch makes me believe that the SCP is the radio signals, however your hook contradicts this and makes me think it is the abnormal mass.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My bad, clarification should have been added. The main anomaly is the signals, the mass is a secondary anomaly I.E. SCP-XXXX-1.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My second question would be how does an astronomer notices this mass? You clearly state that the signals are originating from a galaxy, implying that the foundation can’t narrow it down any further than that; but then you contradict this by saying an astronomer notices a mass exiting the galaxy. For one, the astronomer would barely be able to see the galaxy through the dust plane in the Milky Way, let alone notice a mass heading towards earth, and the mass would have to be absolutely massive for the scientist to see it. And something that big would collapse in on itself due to its own gravitational pull.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This part of your critique, although understandable and will result in adjustments down the line, I feel is not necessarily that big of a problem. The foundation has access to insane technology, both anomalous and non-anomalous, so it isn't too far fetched to believe that they have the means to spot the mass.</p>
<p>I also don't feel that the astronomer noticing the mass exiting the galaxy contradicts anything to do with the signals? There's a stark contrast to invisible radio signals travelling through space and a massive mass exiting a galaxy. I do see your point, however.</p>
<p>As for the mass collapsing in on itself, it can fairly easily be explained as an anomalous effect (although a cheap explanation, it works).</p>
<p>Nice critique! Some parts felt nit picky but there is a good bit of validity.</p>

