<p>One thing I learned real early writing on the wiki is that there's a direct trade-off between mystery and amount of story told, and your way too far to one side of that equation.</p>
<p>Regarding your specific idea, you're thinking way, way too small. There's barely anything <em>happening</em> here. If you think about this like a movie, you've got the pre-credits scene, maybe the first act if we're being generous. They detect a signal, do exactly what you would expect them to, and we discover some <em>extremely vague</em> mysterious info about the threat. There's nowhere near enough there for us readers to sink our teeth into, so we aren't going to care enough to fill in the gaps and be scared.</p>
<p>You need to ask yourself at least three really big <em>What next?</em>'s. And I'm not talking small answers, like &quot;the mass is quivering.&quot; I'm talking <em>big</em> answer, like &quot;IT GETS HERE&quot; big, or &quot;we discover some really cool or strange <em>unexpected</em> thing about the senders/object/universe&quot; big. That is the scale you're operating on with extraterrestrial SCPs.</p>
<p>Like, in terms of existing SCPs, sometimes the answer to that question was &quot;we find a planet that died as a god was born and all that's left is its massive corpse&quot;, or &quot;there was an entire civilisation that was deeply inspired by us and died out, but wanted to thank us&quot;, or &quot;ancient humanity slew aliens and destroyers a giant geodesic sphere around Earth,&quot; or &quot;the signal led to an invasion of earth so bad that we had to put 055 in 579, told in a dozen successive documents.&quot;</p>
<p>(Each of those had mysterious elements as well.)</p>
<p>BIG answers.</p>
<p>Push yourself. A lot. You've barely started.</p>

